      ⍝ only checks that ⎕PLOT was compiled
      ⍝
      ⎕PLOT 0

      )MORE
NO )MORE ERROR INFO

      ⍝ Bugs11 #5 (Blake McBride, 2026-08-04): monadic ⎕PLOT lacked the
      ⍝ element_count<2 guard that the dyadic form has, so a 0-row
      ⍝ argument reached setup_data() with row_count==0; Plot_data's six
      ⍝ min/max members were then read uninitialized by
      ⍝ Plot_window_properties (UB, occasionally a crash).
      ⍝
      ⎕PLOT 0 5⍴0
LENGTH ERROR
      ⎕PLOT 0 5⍴0
      ^

      ⎕PLOT 0 0⍴0
LENGTH ERROR
      ⎕PLOT 0 0⍴0
      ^

      ⍝ clear the SI left behind by the LENGTH ERRORs above
      ⍝
      )SIC

      ⍝ regression (found while auditing get_cravel() call sites after
      ⍝ Blake McBride's Bugs12.md #1, 2026-08-07): setup_data_3D()/2D()
      ⍝ fetched cX/cY(/cZ) from the SAME packed B via three (or two)
      ⍝ consecutive no-cache get_cravel() calls before reading any of
      ⍝ them -- each fetch silently overwrote B's single shared
      ⍝ ravel.cell_fetch_cache, so by the time X[p]/Y[p](/Z[p]) were
      ⍝ finally read, cX/cY(/cZ) all aliased the LAST fetch (cZ for the
      ⍝ 3-plane case, cY for the 2-plane case) -- silent wrong RESULTS,
      ⍝ not a crash, and only for B large enough to be packed (small B
      ⍝ stays RPT_CELLS and is unaffected). Fixed by giving each
      ⍝ coordinate its own explicit cache cell (get_cravel(idx, cache)).
      ⍝
      ⍝ X increasing, Y decreasing (anti-diagonal "\"): confirmed live
      ⍝ that the pre-fix code rendered an ascending "/" here instead
      ⍝ (X wrongly aliased to Y's descending values).
      ⍝
      X←⍳1000
      Y←1000-⍳1000
      B←2 2 500⍴X,Y
      Z←('gui_driver: ASCII' 'terminal_rows: 10' 'terminal_cols: 40') ⎕PLOT B
      ⎕←Z[1;;]
